A||||11||Motorhead||No Spark!||||1||
Z||000000||Motorhead||10-28-2000||05:29 AM||||While driving in city traffic the other day I noticed that under hard acceleration my 1986, 2.5 coupe was missing badly.  This was immediately followed by the complete termination of all engine function.  After getting the car home I found that there was no ignition.  I have replaced the coil and ignition module to no avail.  The pick-up coil on the distibutor was replaced last year and tests OK.  The lead between the coil and distributor checks out with no breaks and the coil is getting power (yellow wire on the harness is hot).  <BR>Computer? <BR>Any help is greatly appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-28-2000||08:05 AM||||It sounds like you have checked all the things I would have check.  Look to see if any fuses have blown.  I would have guessed the coil wire, because that what the symtoms sound like.  But if the coil wire is good???<BR> When you get the problem solved, post the repair.||||reg||5
Z||000002||Seanh||10-28-2000||09:09 AM||||I had a nasty problem like that a while back. How does your car idle? My ideas would either be, bad distributor, maybe rusted or bent prongs. Or, your rotor might be loose, check it again to make sure, be sure to push it down til you hear it click into place.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||10-28-2000||10:34 AM||||Since you've checked the normal things here's some off the wall idea's.<BR>The spring in the distributor cap that holds the tip down on the center of the rotor.<BR>How was the tach acting prior to it shutting down? The tach filter could be shorted.<BR>You noted you have power to the coil, is the ground side going to ground? <BR>Did you check the spark plug wires? If one is chaffed or damaged and shorting straight to ground it could cause no spark. To see if you have a short on the spark plug wire side, the rotor would have strange arc lines around it.<BR>Just some of the more "off-the-wall" stuff I have seen happen.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-28-2000||01:40 PM||||The tach filter doesn't have to be a dead short to kill the ignition. Disconnect it. See if it starts.<P>The ignition module or the pickup is the most likely issue <B>IF</B> the distributor is turning. <P>If the dist is not turning and you pull it only to find the gear is good and firmly pinned to the shaft then the cam gears have let go.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Motorhead||10-29-2000||06:06 AM||||I rebuilt this engine last year and it has run with few problems since.  At that time I dissassembled and rebuilt the distributor also.  <BR>The tach filter crossed my mind but I've never had any idle problems to speak of, or wild tach readings.  Where is the tach filter connection you mentioned located Ogre?  I'll try that next.  Distributor IS turning, and cap and rotor show no unusual markings of arcing or wear.  Plug wires are a year old, premium in quality and show no signs of chafing.<BR>Thanks for all the input ya'll!!<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||10-29-2000||07:38 AM||||Way back when.........<BR>I had a '77 Z-28 that did that and it was a bad rotor, (was shorting to the distributor shaft) worth a shot. There is also a fuse link at the P500 plug which feeds either the module or the coil you may wish to check (I can't remember which).||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-29-2000||11:46 AM||||I think the filter is up by the thermostat on the 4 cyl. Don't know for sure. 87's don't have it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Motorhead||11-19-2000||09:06 AM||||New Genuine GM Distributor fixed the problem.<BR>$300!!! OUCH!!<BR>Talked to a couple of mechanics about "de-magnetizing" problems on these units and bit the bullett.  This new unit is supposed to be an updated designe. Good for another 150,000 miles I guess!<BR>Thanks for all the input guys.<BR>  The education continues.....<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||turbo86se||11-19-2000||11:22 AM||||Mike, how've you been? Did you move or are you still at the beach? <P>If you still need a good mechanic LMK!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||DavidHenshaw||11-19-2000||11:43 AM||||Glad you got it fixed. I had similar symptoms in my 86 gt v6 and changing out the distributor with one from Advance Auto, about $145, fixed my problem. The old dist had rusted prongs and the small copper coil in the distributor looked bad with a couple burned spots on it.<P>Dave ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Motorhead||11-20-2000||06:41 AM||||Hey Joel.  Got a job offer at the beach I couldn't refuse so I'm still here.  Been busy with that but getting caught back up with the Fiero.  I've got new e-mail and will be in touch with the Carolina group soon.<P>David- I know I could have gotten the distributor cheaper but I've become a real believer in original GM parts here lately.  A couple of bad experiences with some aftermarket parts.||||reg||1
